1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,080
The library will definitely close.

2
00:00:02,540 --> 00:00:04,280
That's our student's first answer.

3
00:00:04,860 --> 00:00:05,980
Let's put it to the test.

4
00:00:06,800 --> 00:00:09,340
Counterfactual Worlds turns history and literature

5
00:00:09,220 --> 00:00:11,300
into places students can explore.

6
00:00:12,520 --> 00:00:14,240
Teachers start with thirty prepared lessons,

7
00:00:14,800 --> 00:00:15,540
review their sources,

8
00:00:15,700 --> 00:00:18,300
and bring real museum objects into the classroom.

9
00:00:19,440 --> 00:00:21,880
In Alexandria, collapse harbor trade.

10
00:00:22,560 --> 00:00:23,120
Explore the city.

11
00:00:23,840 --> 00:00:25,940
Compare what changes and what we're only assuming.

12
00:00:26,980 --> 00:00:28,400
Read Strabo in context.

13
00:00:28,980 --> 00:00:29,560
Save evidence.

14
00:00:29,960 --> 00:00:31,140
Build an argument.

15
00:00:31,980 --> 00:00:34,340
Astra challenges claims that go beyond the sources.

16
00:00:35,360 --> 00:00:36,740
The teacher adds a new question.

17
00:00:37,260 --> 00:00:39,420
Could a replacement patron support the scholars?

18
00:00:39,860 --> 00:00:42,080
The student keeps their work and revises.

19
00:00:43,500 --> 00:00:46,220
Now, closure is possible, not inevitable.

20
00:00:47,020 --> 00:00:49,680
Teachers see the first and latest explanations together.

21
00:00:50,100 --> 00:00:53,300
Our goal: richer learning, whatever the budget.

22
00:00:53,700 --> 00:00:55,150
Built with Astra.

23
00:00:55,650 --> 00:00:56,950
Counterfactual Worlds.

24
00:00:57,200 --> 00:00:58,350
Step inside a question.

25
00:00:58,750 --> 00:00:59,950
Come back with an argument.
